# Nested projection JSON response

Use the nested projection JSON response pattern for every screen.

A screen makes one GET and renders that JSON. The browser does not call another route for the same view, and it does not join tables.

The response is one nested document. A related record is an object inside its parent. Every key inside an object is a column name from that table, in snake_case. Return only the columns that screen shows. Zod in the feature `types/index.ts` checks each object against those columns.

Do not invent keys. There is no `title`, `amount`, `price`, `stage`, `snippet`, or `name` unless that column exists. `deals.status` is the stage. `customers.full_name` is the name. A quote has `quote_number`, not a name or a price. A count is allowed only when the screen shows a count and no column stores it, as `unread_count` does.

The route stays thin: parse the query, call `server/queries.ts`, return JSON. The query batches its reads and builds the document. Do not load one related row per parent.

Opening another record fetches that record once. Do not load the list again. A list that also opens the first record uses one response, `GET /api/crm/conversations?autoSelect=first` returning `{ conversations, conversation }`.

```json
{
  "deals": [
    {
      "id": "deal_302",
      "name": "Deal 1",
      "source": "quote",
      "status": "active_quote",
      "customers": [{ "id": "cust_904", "full_name": "Harper Quinn" }],
      "quote": {
        "id": "q_88",
        "quote_number": 12,
        "status": "sent",
        "quote_revision": { "id": "rev_3", "revision_number": 3, "status": "sent" }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

Writes and event streams stay on their own routes. The public quote page keeps its existing document.
